This is the Geneva optimization library. As the name suggests
-- it stands for "Grid-enabled evolutionary algorithms" --, it
aims to provide the necessary tools to perform parametric optimization
in parallel on devices ranging from multi-processor machines over
clusters all the way to Grid- and Cloud-installations.

Meanwhile, further optimization algorithms have been added beyond "mere"
evolutionary algorithms, such as particle swarm optimization, simulated
annealing, a simple form of gradient descents and an exhaustive parameter scan.
Further algorithms will be added over time, and the existing algorithms will be
further improved.

All algorithms act on the same problem definitions, and different
optimization algorithms can interact and may be "chained", making it
easy to tackle even complicated problems. Parallelization (on the level
of optimization algorithms) happens mostly transparently for the user.

Geneva does not want to be the fastest library of optimization
algorithms on small machines, but is instead targeted at
large scale problems, where the evaluation of a single individual will
typically take longer than a few seconds.

Geneva is made available as an Open Source software, under the Apache
License, version 2.0. External contributions to the code are very
welcome, and indeed we make this code available in the hope that it
will not only be useful, but will also encourage users to let us know
about bugs or suggest or even implement features they would want to
see in Geneva.


Building and installing
=======================

Geneva requires an out-of-source build with a C++23-capable compiler and
Boost >= 1.91. The recommended system is Ubuntu 26.10, whose package archive
carries a CUDA toolkit and NVIDIA driver recent enough for the optional GPU
features. See the INSTALL file for the prerequisites (compilers, Boost,
CMake, optional CUDA / MPI / ROOT), the supported processors, and step-by-step
build instructions using either the prepareBuild.sh convenience script or a
direct cmake call.

Note: version 2.0 is a ground-up redesign relative to the 1.x series. If you are
upgrading existing problem definitions, configuration files or checkpoints,
read docs/CHANGES-2.0.md first -- it collects the source- and
serialization-breaking changes and how to adapt to them.


Documentation
=============

A reference manual can be generated from the Geneva sources with the command
"make doc", once CMake has been set up. As with the normal compilation, run it
from the chosen (out-of-source) build directory. Generating the reference
documentation requires a full Doxygen installation (including its tools) and,
for the typeset output, a LaTeX system.

The quickstart in projects/geneva/quickstart is the best practical starting
point for writing your own problems: a self-contained project that consumes an
installed Geneva via find_package(Geneva) and can be copied out of this tree.
See also docs/writing-optimization-problems.md and the examples shipped under
each library's examples/ directory.


Contributing
============

External contributions are very welcome. Please contact the authors on how to
contribute to the Geneva library collection, and report bugs or issues through
the Geneva GitHub project at https://github.com/gemfony/geneva-suite/issues .
